00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.550
And now, back to the show.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.800
Alright guys, let’s go in to a little recap.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.590
Peter Gibbons is a dissatisfied programmer
at an oppressively generic tech firm, where

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.340
he spends his days getting nagged by his boss.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.471
At his girlfriend’s request, he agrees to
see an “occupational hypnotherapist” who

00:02:27.471 --> 00:02:32.400
puts Peter in a trance and then promptly dies
of a heart attack.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:39.340
Peter’s left in a state of blissful non-give-a-fuckery,
which he then pairs with flip-flops and heads

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:40.530
into the office.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:45.569
When the firm’s new consultants meet a suddenly-chillaxed
Peter, they’re so impressed by his boss-like

00:02:45.569 --> 00:02:48.230
attitude that they literally make him one.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:54.430
"So you're gonna fire Micheal and Samir and
you're gonna give me more money?"

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:57.400
"Wow"

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.530
Once Peter’s promoted, he and his buddies
plot to rob their company and things go a

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:02.530
little nuts.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:05.849
Now, for the purposes of this video we’re
not going to be talking much about the scam

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:07.590
that fuels the second half of the film.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:11.670
Rather, we’re focusing on the film’s legacy
as a cultural artifact that speaks to employee

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:14.590
disaffection and general workplace ambivalence.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:18.740
People don’t proudly display an Office Space
poster to express their desire to steal millions

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:23.620
from their corporate overlords- well maybe
that-but mostly to encapsulate their fantasy

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:26.239
of showing their boss how little they care.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:30.920
To understand why Office Space is so intoxicating,
we need to take a super-brief look at the

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:34.080
history of the office as a social construct.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.790
But not the physical office, but say, “The
Office of the Mayor.”

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:42.489
As philosopher Giorgio Agamben recounts in
his book Opus Dei: An Archaeology of Duty,

00:03:42.489 --> 00:03:46.580
the idea of the office really began with the
institutionalization of the Catholic church.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:51.349
If a priest tells you to do 100 Hail Marys,
well that could be just some rando arbitrarily

00:03:51.349 --> 00:03:52.520
making up a number.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:56.920
But coming from a guy holding an official
office with ties to the official church, it

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.040
gives his order more legitimacy.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:04.000
Which is all well and good, but this construct
of the office has had rippling effects.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.720
Scholar Benjamin Lewis Robinson builds off
Agamben by suggesting that, from the beginning,

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.769
this imbued the culture of work with a spiritual
and transcendent element.

00:04:12.769 --> 00:04:16.900
By early modern history, Robinson writes,
“the notion of the efficacy of the office

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:22.280
was generalized into a moral concept that
defined the very agency of human beings.”

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:26.210
Doing your work wasn’t just about a paycheck,
it was about duty and purpose.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:31.120
In other words, your very “you-ness” is
defined by what you do for work, and how effectively

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.120
you do it.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.160
Now, This may seem like a given, but that
just shows how thoroughly our society buys

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.240
into the idea.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:43.180
In this way, our very conception of ourselves
is defined by our obligations as employees,

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:44.180
which sucks.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:48.650
The phenomenon of a person’s work subsuming
their identity is pretty clearly illustrated

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:54.150
in Office Space when Peter is casually asked
by his boss to work not just one, but both

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:55.240
days of the weekend.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:04.190
"I'm gonna need you to go ahead and come in
tomorrow, so if you could be here around 9

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:08.580
that would be great, m'kay?

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:16.500
Oh-oh and I almost forgot ah- I'm also gonna
need you to go ahead and come in on Sunday

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.370
too, kay?"

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:21.449
Because why would he have a life outside of
his job?

00:05:21.449 --> 00:05:26.360
Similarly, for his soon-to-be-boo Joanna,
every part of her identity is regulated by

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:27.530
her waitressing job.

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:32.120
Her “flair,” or assortment of buttons
and patches might seem to be showcasing her

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:33.120
personality.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.520
But, we soon learn that such displays of individuality
are actually mandated by her boss.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.100
"We need to talk about your flair" "Really?

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:47.430
I have 15 pieces on" "Well 15 is the minuimum,
okay?"

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:53.810
Here, her very performance of selfhood is
tightly controlled by her job.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:58.389
So we have this monolithic definition of the
office as a place where we not only accomplish

00:05:58.389 --> 00:06:01.600
our life’s work, but our lives as such.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.509
Is it any wonder we might come to resent the
very institution?

00:06:04.509 --> 00:06:09.160
Indeed, the slacker lifestyle didn’t begin
with your cool incense-burning aunt.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:14.400
In fact, we see a distinctly-Office-Spacian-brand
of slacktivism fermenting in author Herman

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:19.919
Melville’s iconic 1848 short story, “Bartleby
the Scrivener: A Story of Wall Street,”

00:06:19.919 --> 00:06:24.900
which we have opted to illustrate vis-a-vis
a delightfully corny 1969 short film adaptation

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:27.410
because, those eyebrows…

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:31.729
Melville’s story chronicles, through the
eyes of a bewildered lawyer, the exploits

00:06:31.729 --> 00:06:37.259
of his newest employee, a scribe named Bartleby,
who, after working industriously for several

00:06:37.259 --> 00:06:42.240
weeks copying lengthy law documents, suddenly
decides that he doesn’t feel like it anymore.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.509
His vernacular choice has become immortalized
in literature and pop culture.

00:06:46.509 --> 00:06:50.960
When asked to do anything he doesn’t want
to do, Bartleby simply says “I'd prefer

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:51.960
not to.”

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:55.710
This starts with not wanting to proofread
his copy, then escalates to refusing to copy

00:06:55.710 --> 00:07:00.100
at all, and eventually culminates in him refusing
to leave the law office, where he just plain

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:03.410
prefers to hang out all night while quietly
staring out the window.

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:07.960
The similarities between Bartleby and Peter’s
initial narratives are not insignificant.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:11.210
Both are low-level employees working for passive-aggressive
bosses.

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:15.130
Both labor in claustrophobic solitude doing
work they don’t care about.

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:19.410
Both appear to have a distaste for bureaucracy
And both will eventually seek solace in the

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:23.590
great outdoors, to varying degrees of happiness,
but we’ll get to that later.

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:28.150
Most importantly, both turn their offices
upside down when they decide to stop working.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:33.169
But let’s start by looking at their defining
characteristic: The choice not to work.

00:07:33.169 --> 00:07:37.300
According to Robinson, Bartleby’s refusal
to act is not a “mere inaction.”

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:42.470
Rather, by “preferring” not to act, Bartleby
ventures from sheer passivity into what he

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:47.500
calls a “second-order passivity” which
is “performed in its ineffectiveness.”

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:53.199
He, and Peter as well, choose to be useless,
in the very place where effectiveness is the

00:07:53.199 --> 00:07:54.880
highest calling.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:59.099
So inexplicable is Peter’s behavior, and
his apparent unwillingness to do his damn

00:07:59.099 --> 00:08:05.620
job, that the uncomprehending, chummy consultants
decide it must be evidence of superiority:

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:10.090
He is, indeed, too good for his work, and
thus should be promoted.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:13.940
Peter’s inaction becomes even more radical
when considered with the culture of office

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:14.940
politics.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:19.220
As Robinson puts it, “The modern office
is the space that ensures that it is easy

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:23.430
to do one’s duty” free from the burden
of your conscience or the consequences of

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:24.430
your actions.

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:29.590
When your work is inherently disengaging,
he adds, “petty conflicts and trivial confrontations

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:31.720
seem to take on epic proportions.”

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:36.279
This, he notes would eventually manifest delightfully
as “office politics,” or the cultural

00:08:36.279 --> 00:08:41.789
science of why the HELL Cassidy chews her
Greek salad so damn loud.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:45.741
Office politics are on full display in Office
Space, whether in the form of Peter’s boss

00:08:45.741 --> 00:08:50.709
claiming the best parking spot, or poor Milton
being separated from his beloved stapler and

00:08:50.709 --> 00:08:53.679
relegated to increasingly worse desk assignments.

00:08:53.679 --> 00:08:59.160
By refusing to engage in or even recognize
office politics - as when Peter steals his

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:04.510
boss’s precious parking spot, stops dressing
up, and comes in when he feels like it - a

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:10.600
newly-zen Peter acts like an anarchist agitating
against the very institution of the office.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:15.329
Bartleby and Peter bring something else profound
to the office, as identified in the famous

00:09:15.329 --> 00:09:17.439
last line of Melville’s story.

00:09:17.439 --> 00:09:21.601
After Bartleby inevitably starves to death
in prison after preferring not to eat, his

00:09:21.601 --> 00:09:26.639
former boss exclaims: “Ah Bartleby.

00:09:26.639 --> 00:09:29.379
Ah humanity.”

00:09:29.379 --> 00:09:33.089
Robinson argues that this line can be read
as a recognition of the fact that Bartleby,

00:09:33.089 --> 00:09:38.191
in all his perverse peculiarity, is “what
humanity would look like in an office, were

00:09:38.191 --> 00:09:40.249
it ever to make such an appearance.”

00:09:40.249 --> 00:09:44.800
In other words, for an office to function,
it must strip us of said humanity.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:49.679
In Bartleby’s refusal to do boring grunt
work, his desire to do nothing and his complete

00:09:49.679 --> 00:09:54.540
rejection of the mundanities of office life,
he displays an unwillingness to suppress his

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:57.370
human desires for the sake of official duties.

00:09:57.370 --> 00:10:02.149
Similarly, tranced-out Peter seems to embody
the selfish, pleasure-seeking impulses that

00:10:02.149 --> 00:10:07.470
unite us in humanity - he acts on base human
desires in a world where everyone else simply

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:12.129
ignores or represses them beneath a veneer
of earnest good will and productivity.

00:10:12.129 --> 00:10:17.079
A human version of Peter looks like a guy
who actually dates someone he likes, a guy

00:10:17.079 --> 00:10:22.529
who watches kung fu movies, a guy who smashes
a faulty printer just for the heck of it.

00:10:22.529 --> 00:10:26.230
It’s worth noting that these two slackers
are backed by one influential philosopher

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:27.820
- Slavoj Zizek.

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:32.820
Zizek is a major proponent of Bartleby’s
style of Political Action, and is one of modernity’s

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:38.189
biggest advocates for saying “I would prefer
not to” to… well, everything.

00:10:38.189 --> 00:10:41.610
Indeed, inaction, he argues, can be incredibly
active.

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:46.949
Zizek wrote in his book Violence that, “Sometimes
doing nothing is the most violent thing to

00:10:46.949 --> 00:10:47.949
do.”

00:10:47.949 --> 00:10:50.899
If this sounds ridiculous, you’ve clearly
never been ghosted.

00:10:50.899 --> 00:10:54.430
But why is Zizek wearing the jersey of literature’s
greatest slacker?

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:58.809
To understand that, we first need to contextualize
Zizek’s perspective on contemporary society.

00:10:58.809 --> 00:11:03.239
His argument goes like this: We live in a
world of economic and political deadlock where

00:11:03.239 --> 00:11:08.029
multinational corporations have all the power
and change feels low key impossible.

00:11:08.029 --> 00:11:12.809
This is the net result of the 20th century,
which was a time of intense action - various

00:11:12.809 --> 00:11:18.129
countries experimented with socialism, facism,
communism, hippie-ism, bad-hair-ism [80s],

00:11:18.129 --> 00:11:19.389
every ism they could think of.

00:11:19.389 --> 00:11:24.040
It brought change, much of it not great, and
resulted in our present day world.

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:28.620
In Zizek’s mind, all that action may have
been misguided, or as he puts it “maybe

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:30.939
we tried to change the world too quickly.”

00:11:30.939 --> 00:11:36.410
And so we’re stuck with status quo capitalism,
rampant inequality, the Amazon on fire, and

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:39.870
Cassidy not shutting up about essential oils.

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:44.459
Along those lines, Zizek tends to think that,
despite capitalism’s numerous flaws, we

00:11:44.459 --> 00:11:47.170
don’t yet have an alternative worth actualizing.

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:52.249
As a result, any activity that we do take
is not only doomed to fail, but actually risks

00:11:52.249 --> 00:11:53.899
aiding the powers that be.

00:11:53.899 --> 00:11:58.549
Zizek warns against engaging in “localised
acts whose ultimate function is to make the

00:11:58.549 --> 00:12:00.319
system run more smoothly.”

00:12:00.319 --> 00:12:06.799
He cautions that, rather than fearing passivity,
we should strenuously work to avoid “pseudo-activity”

00:12:06.799 --> 00:12:10.889
that is “the urge to ‘be active’ to
“participate,” simply as a way “to mask”

00:12:10.889 --> 00:12:12.549
the pointlessness of your actions.”

00:12:12.549 --> 00:12:17.149
That right there describes the office work
both Bartleby and Peter rail against.

00:12:17.149 --> 00:12:21.679
In refusing to copy legal documents or print
out cover letters for boring reports, they

00:12:21.679 --> 00:12:26.699
are boldly asserting a truth that only doing
nothing can communicate: that the office work

00:12:26.699 --> 00:12:30.929
which once dominated their lives is actually
totally meaningless.

00:12:30.929 --> 00:12:35.449
While it may be tempting to tell your boss
off, that may only lead to new new HR rules,

00:12:35.449 --> 00:12:39.399
you may complain of overwork, but they’ll
just add a pool table and call themselves

00:12:39.399 --> 00:12:43.889
heros, or you may want to burn the place down,
but that will only put a fat insurance check

00:12:43.889 --> 00:12:45.499
in your boss’s pocket.

00:12:45.499 --> 00:12:48.399
All these things only make the system stronger.

00:12:48.399 --> 00:12:51.839
The most radical form of rebellion may be
to do nothing.

00:12:51.839 --> 00:12:56.019
Lastly, it’s important to look at the ending
of both Office Space and Bartleby.

00:12:56.019 --> 00:12:58.920
What is the net result of each main character’s
defiance?

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:02.790
In Office Space, the office is burnt down
and Peter takes a job cleaning up the refuge

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:04.329
with his next door neighbor.

00:13:04.329 --> 00:13:07.879
“This isn’t so bad, huh?

00:13:07.879 --> 00:13:12.119
Making bucks, getting exercise, working outside."

00:13:12.119 --> 00:13:16.970
In retreating from office life entirely, he’s
sought a return to blue collar labor, getting

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:22.339
as close to a pastoral paradise of sweat,
calluses, hard work, and tangible results

00:13:22.339 --> 00:13:24.589
as modern city life might allow.

00:13:24.589 --> 00:13:28.859
For Bartleby, his refusal to leave the office
eventually lands him in prison, where he spends

00:13:28.859 --> 00:13:31.199
all his time hanging out in the courtyard.

00:13:31.199 --> 00:13:35.040
Fittingly enough, he too has escaped the grind
and fled to the outdoors.

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:39.869
Sure, he starves himself to death, but at
least he didn’t die at his desk, right?

00:13:39.869 --> 00:13:41.100
So, what do you guys think?

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:45.500
Does your effectiveness at work or school
define you, and how do you feel about that?

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:50.189
Do you fantasize about doing nothing like
Zizek, Peter and Bartleby, or is preferring

00:13:50.189 --> 00:13:51.600
not to just a cop out?

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:52.959
Let us know what you think in the comments.

00:13:52.959 --> 00:13:57.009
Thanks to all our awesome patrons for supporting
our channel and our podcasts.

00:13:57.009 --> 00:14:09.549
Go ahead and hit that “Subscribe” button,
and as always, thanks for watching guys.

00:14:09.549 --> 00:14:18.979
Peace!

